The task is to compute the Highest Common Factor (HCF) and Least Common Multiple (LCM) for two given positive integers. A common formula relates the two values:
  LCM(a, b) = (a × b) / HCF(a, b)

Key constraints include:

Both input numbers are positive integers.
Inputs might be large, so care must be taken to avoid integer overflow when computing the product.
Zero should be handled appropriately if it occurs as input.
Section 2 — Intuition
The optimal method for computing the HCF of two numbers is to use the Euclidean algorithm, which is based on the principle that HCF(a, b) is the same as HCF(b, a mod b). Once the HCF is determined, the relation between HCF and LCM is used to compute the LCM. This method is efficient because the Euclidean algorithm runs in logarithmic time relative to the input sizes.

Section 3 — Approach 1: Brute Force
A naive approach to finding the HCF is to iterate from the minimum of the two numbers downwards and find the first number that evenly divides both. Once the HCF is found, the LCM can be computed using the product divided by the HCF.

Copy
cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

// Brute force approach to compute HCF
int bruteForceHCF(int a, int b) {
    int hcf = 1;
    int minVal = (a < b) ? a : b;
    for (int i = minVal; i >= 1; i--) {
        // Check if i divides both a and b
        if (a % i == 0 && b % i == 0) {
            hcf = i;
            break; // first common factor from the top will be the highest
        }
    }
    return hcf;
}

int main() {
    int a = 48, b = 180;
    int hcf = bruteForceHCF(a, b);
    // To avoid overflow, ensure division is done before multiplication when possible.
    int lcm = (a * b) / hcf;
    cout << "Brute Force Approach:" << endl;
    cout << "HCF: " << hcf << ", LCM: " << lcm << endl;
    return 0;
}
Time Complexity: O(min(a, b)) — since we potentially iterate from the smaller number down to 1.
Space Complexity: O(1) — only constant space is used.

Section 4 — Approach 2: Optimal Solution
The optimal approach uses the Euclidean algorithm to compute HCF. Then, using the formula LCM = (a × b) / HCF, the LCM is computed. This method is both efficient and straightforward.

Step-by-step algorithm:

Use a loop or recursion to compute HCF using the Euclidean algorithm.
Compute LCM by multiplying a and b, then dividing by the computed HCF.
Copy
cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

// Optimal approach using the Euclidean algorithm for HCF
int euclideanHCF(int a, int b) {
    while (b != 0) {
        int remainder = a % b;
        a = b;
        b = remainder;
    }
    return a;
}

int main() {
    int a = 48, b = 180;
    int hcf = euclideanHCF(a, b);
    // Use the relation to compute LCM. Caution: For very large a, b, multiplication might overflow.
    int lcm = (a * b) / hcf;
    cout << "Optimal Approach:" << endl;
    cout << "HCF: " << hcf << ", LCM: " << lcm << endl;
    return 0;
}
Time Complexity: O(log(min(a, b))) — due to the efficiency of the Euclidean algorithm.
Space Complexity: O(1) — only constant space is used.

