Section 6 — Detailed Dry Run
Take a = 48 and b = 180 using the optimal approach (Euclidean algorithm):

Step	a	b	Calculation	Explanation
1	48	180	180 % 48 = 36	180 divided by 48 leaves a remainder of 36
2	180	36	48 % 36 = 12	Now a becomes 48 and b becomes 36
3	36	12	36 % 12 = 0	36 divided by 12 leaves no remainder
4	12	0	End loop	HCF is 12
Then, LCM = (48 × 180) / 12 = 8640 / 12 = 720.

Section 7 — Edge Cases
Input: a = 0, b = any positive number
  Expected: Proper handling (often defined as HCF(0, x) = x and LCM = 0)
Input: a = b
  Expected: HCF and LCM both equal to the same number
Input: a and b are prime numbers
  Expected: HCF = 1; LCM = a × b
Input: Very large numbers
  Expected: Avoid integer overflow during multiplication
Input: Negative numbers (if allowed)
  Expected: Typically take absolute values before computation
Section 8 — Common Mistakes
Not handling zero properly:
  • Mistake: Failing to check if one of the numbers is zero, which can lead to division by zero or incorrect results.
  • Fix: Add checks to define HCF(0, x) as x and handle LCM accordingly.

Incorrect usage of division in the LCM formula:
  • Mistake: Multiplying a and b first without considering potential overflow before dividing by the HCF.
  • Fix: Consider using data types that can handle large integers or rearrange the computation if possible.

Poor implementation of the Euclidean algorithm:
  • Mistake: Not properly updating variables inside the loop or missing the base case in recursion.
  • Fix: Carefully implement the while loop or recursive function with correct termination conditions.

Using a brute force approach for large numbers:
  • Mistake: Iterating from the minimum of the two numbers downwards, which is inefficient for large numbers.
  • Fix: Use the Euclidean algorithm to achieve a logarithmic time complexity.

Overlooking edge cases in input validation:
  • Mistake: Assuming both inputs are positive or neglecting to consider special cases such as when the numbers are equal.
  • Fix: Validate inputs and include tests for edge cases to ensure robustness.

